                <pb facs="0845" n="805" xml:id="pg805"/>

                <head>397. Memorandum of a Conversation, Department of State, Washington, October
                    28, 1956, 4:49 p.m.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d397-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            type="conversation-or-meeting-date" when="1956-10-28T16:49:00-05:00"><hi
                                rendition="#italic">October 28, 1956, 4:49
                        p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>Reported Israeli Mobilization</item>
                </list>
                <list subtype="participants" type="description">
                    <label>PARTICIPANTS</label>
                    <item>The Secretary of State</item>
                    <item>Mr. Alphand, French Ambassador</item>
                    <item>Mr. J. E. Coulson, British Chargé</item>
                    <item>The Under Secretary of State</item>
                    <item>Mr. Murphy, G</item>
                    <item>Mr. Rountree, NEA</item>
                    <item>Mr. Beam, EUR</item>
                </list>
                <p>The Secretary saw the French Ambassador and the British Chargé after they had
                    left Mr. Murphy’s office.</p>
                <p>The Secretary reiterated our great concern about the latest developments in
                    Israel, referring to the President’s appeal to Ben Gurion. He mentioned that the
                    timing of the Israeli mobilization was very ominous. The Israelis might be
                    thinking that a number of factors offered a favorable opportunity to start
                    military action: the Suez dispute, the situation in Jordan and the American
                    elections. They might be counting upon our reluctance to take strong measures
                    during the last week of the elections; if so, they were miscalculating. The
                    Secretary assumed the UK still stood by its obligations under the Jordan
                    treaty.</p>
                <p>The British Chargé said he was convinced this was the case and that his
                    government had said so several times.</p>
                <p>The French Ambassador and British Chargé reiterated that they had had no
                    information from their governments regarding Israeli mobilization measures of a
                    kind similar to that in possession of the US Government. Mr. Coulson mentioned
                    that his government had however made an inquiry in Tel Aviv concerning reports
                    of military preparations, expressing its concern.</p>
                <p>The Secretary said Israel was claiming that developments in Jordan were forcing
                    it to mobilize. He observed that the Negev however seemed far less subject to
                    attack than recently.</p>
                <pb facs="0846" n="806" xml:id="pg806"/>
                <p>The meeting ended with a brief exchange of information on latest events in
                    Hungary.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
